## 6. Versioned API Contract

Two independent version numbers matter, and conflating them is the most common cause of plugin-ecosystem breakage — keep them separate everywhere (manifest, docs, SDK):

- **Plugin version** (`manifest.version`) — the plugin's own release, owned by the team, semver, means nothing to the host.
- **API version** (`manifest.apiVersion`) — which shape of the host↔plugin contract the plugin was built against. This is what the host actually gates on.

### Compatibility policy
- Host publishes API version as `MAJOR.MINOR` (no patch — patch-level fixes to the contract are host-internal bugs, not contract changes).
- **MINOR bumps are additive-only**: new optional RPC methods, new optional manifest fields, new optional extension-point config. A plugin built against `2.1` keeps working unmodified against a host offering `2.3`.
- **MAJOR bumps may remove or change semantics** of existing methods/fields.
- Host supports **N-1 major versions concurrently** (i.e., when host ships API v3, it still runs plugins declaring `apiVersion: 2.x` through a compatibility shim layer) for a minimum deprecation window of **one quarter** — directly matched to your "five teams ship next quarter" cadence so no team is forced into a synchronized migration.
- At `onLoad`, host and plugin perform a handshake: plugin declares `apiVersion`; host either (a) serves it directly if within supported range, (b) serves it through the shim if in the deprecated-but-supported range, or (c) rejects load with a clear `UnsupportedApiVersionError` if too old, logged loudly, surfaced in admin UI with an upgrade link to the changelog.

### Contract artifact
The contract itself is not just prose — it's a versioned schema package the host publishes and all 5 teams depend on:

```
@reporting-tool/plugin-contract
├── v2/
│   ├── rpc-methods.schema.json      # every host->plugin and plugin->host method, JSON Schema per method
│   ├── types.ts                     # generated TS types (if teams use TS)
│   ├── types.py                     # generated Python types (dataclasses/pydantic)
│   └── CHANGELOG.md
└── v3/
    └── ...
```

This package is generated from a single source-of-truth IDL (recommend Protobuf or a shared JSON Schema set) so the TS and Python type definitions can't drift from each other or from what the host actually enforces. CI for the host **fails the build** if a MINOR bump removes a field or a MAJOR bump isn't accompanied by a new versioned directory + updated shim.

### Deprecation workflow
1. Mark a method/field `deprecated: true` + `sunsetApiVersion` in schema.
2. Host logs a structured warning (once per plugin per process lifetime, not per call) when a plugin uses a deprecated surface.
3. Admin UI has a "plugins using deprecated API" report, so the platform team can proactively ping the 1-2 teams still on old surfaces before the deprecation window closes, instead of finding out at cutover time.

