## 7. Permissions & Capability Broker

Already introduced in §5 as the enforcement point; specifying its internal model:

```typescript
interface ResolvedPermissions {
  network: Set<string>;         // exact allowlist, resolved from manifest ∩ admin approval
  filesystem: FsGrant[];
  dataScopes: Set<string>;
  secrets: Set<string>;
}

// Every host-provided callback goes through this check, every call, not just at load.
class CapabilityBroker {
  async invoke(pluginId: string, capability: string, args: unknown) {
    const perms = this.registry.get(pluginId).grantedPermissions;
    if (!this.isAuthorized(capability, args, perms)) {
      this.audit.log({ pluginId, capability, args, decision: "denied" });
      throw new PermissionDeniedError(capability);
    }
    this.audit.log({ pluginId, capability, args, decision: "allowed" });
    return this.hostServices[capability](args);
  }
}
```

- **Admin can grant less than a plugin asks for** (manifest permissions are a *ceiling*, not an entitlement) — installing a plugin that requests `dataScopes: ["reports:read", "reports:write"]` can be approved read-only by an admin; the plugin gets a `LoadResult` reflecting the reduced grant and must degrade gracefully (this is why `onLoad`'s response includes `capabilities` — the plugin confirms what it can actually do with what it got).
- Every capability invocation is audit-logged (pluginId, capability, args digest, allow/deny, timestamp) — this is your answer to "which plugin touched this data" during an incident.

